# CHAPTER 8: "ARRIVAL"

## FIVE YEARS AGO

Silas Ward sat in his office—twenty-third floor, AstraCore tower, corner view of a city that didn't give a shit about him.

His datapad glowed with the accusation: **EMBEZZLEMENT. INTERNAL INVESTIGATION. PENDING TERMINATION.**

Numbers he'd never seen. Accounts he'd never opened. Evidence so clean it had to be fabricated.

But nobody cared about truth. They cared about optics.

His wife's voice still echoed from that morning: *"I can't do this, Silas. The shame. Everyone knows. I'm taking Emma."*

His daughter. Seven years old. Wouldn't look at him when they left.

His mother called an hour later. Heart attack. Stress-induced. Dead before the ambulance arrived.

Three days later: Fired. Blacklisted. Broke.

One week later: Eviction notice.

Two weeks later: Merc work. Because corpo skills translated to violence when you were desperate enough.

Silas stared at the empty apartment—furniture repossessed, family gone, future erased.

He picked up the pistol he'd bought that morning. Considered it. Put it down.

*Not yet. Not like this.*

If he was going to survive, he'd have to become something else.

Something harder.

---

## PRESENT DAY

The checkpoint loomed ahead—reinforced walls, automated turrets, guards with military precision. Ashen Reach didn't welcome visitors.

Kai sat in the passenger seat of Silas's vehicle, Vantage Optics scanning the fortifications. Twelve guards visible. Probably more inside. Heavy chrome, professional stance. This wasn't VSD—this was military remnant.

"Papers. Credentials. Purpose of entry."

The guard's voice crackled through the intercom. Rifle raised. Not threatening yet. Just ready.

Silas leaned toward the speaker: "We're here on business. Private contract."

"Name of contractor?"

Silence. They didn't have a name. Vera's intel had gotten them this far, but not through checkpoints.

The guard's tone shifted: "No response. Hold position for inspection."

Another voice through radio: "Three unknowns. No clearance. No credentials. Protocol?"

Static pause.

"Detain or eliminate."

Kai's hand drifted toward his concealed pistol. Beside him, Dax's fingers tightened on the wheel. In the back, Silas calculated odds.

Not good.

Guards moved into formation—firing line, overlapping fields, professional spacing. Atlas training, or something close.

"Step out of the vehicle. Slowly."

Kai's Surge hummed beneath conscious thought. The Voice quiet but present: *"THREAT ASSESSMENT: TWELVE HOSTILES. OPTIMAL RESPONSE PROTOCOL AVAILABLE."*

Not now. Not here.

"Out. Now."

Then—radio crackle.

"Hold position. Stand down."

Guards hesitated. Looked toward the checkpoint building.

A figure emerged.

Military stride. Confident. Dangerous in the way a predator is dangerous—not aggressive, just apex.

The guards shifted—not quite fear, but close. Respect born from terror.

Kai's breath caught.

He knew that walk. Knew the way the figure moved—efficient, economical, no wasted motion. Atlas 3rd Expeditionary. The same training. The same instincts.

The figure stopped in front of their vehicle.

Kai saw his face through the windshield.

Time stopped.

**Kieran.**

Alive. Not dead. Not buried. Not gone.

His brother stood three meters away, looking directly at him.

Two years. Two years of searching, grieving, carrying guilt. And here he was.

Kieran walked to the driver's side. Ignored Dax completely. Looked past him to Kai.

Their eyes met.

Kai saw it—the cold calculation, the hardness, the thing Kieran had become. But underneath, something else. Something that flickered and died before Kai could name it.

Kieran turned to the guards: "They're with me. Let them pass."

The lead guard hesitated: "Sir, they have no—"

Kieran's voice dropped ten degrees: "Did I stutter?"

The guard flinched. "No sir! Opening gate!"

Kai opened the door. Couldn't just sit there. Couldn't drive through without—

He stepped out.

Five meters between them.

Brothers separated by time, trauma, and everything unsaid.

"You're alive," Kai said. His voice steadier than he felt.

Kieran tilted his head slightly. "Disappointed?"

"I looked for you. I thought—"

"You thought wrong."

Silence stretched. Guards watching nervously. Dax ready to intervene. Silas assessing.

Kai: "Why are you helping us?"

Kieran's expression didn't change. "Am I?"

He turned to leave.

"Wait—"

Kieran stopped. Looked back over his shoulder. "Drive. Before I change my mind."

He walked away. Guards scrambled to open the gate.

Above, an eagle circled—eyes augmented, optics glowing faint blue. Hayate. Watching everything.

Kai stood there for three seconds too long. Then got back in the vehicle.

"That was your brother?" Dax asked quietly.

"Yeah."

"He just... saved us."

"Maybe."

The gate opened fully. Ashen Reach beyond—neon bleeding into dust, violence and desperation wrapped in city lights.

Kai looked back as they drove through.

Kieran stood at the checkpoint, silhouette against harsh floodlights. Watching them leave.

Or watching them enter his territory.

"We're being watched now," Dax said. "You know that."

Kai touched the pendant under his shirt—Kieran's pendant, the only thing he'd had left.

"I know."

